@page "/help"
@rendermode @(new InteractiveWebAssemblyRenderMode(prerender: false))

@using BgQuiz_Blazor.Client.Quiz
@using XgFilter_Razor.Components

@inject QuizController Controller
@inject NavigationManager Nav

<PageTitle>BgQuiz — Help</PageTitle>

<div class="container py-3" style="max-width:800px">
    <h1>How BgQuiz works</h1>

    <p class="lead">
        BgQuiz turns your own eXtreme Gammon analysis into practice: it shows you a
        position from one of your files, you answer it, and it scores your answer
        against XG's evaluation of that position.
    </p>

    @* Prerequisites, ahead of the flow. BgQuiz ships no sample files and does
       not work on every browser, so a reader can arrive with nothing it can
       act on — and everything below assumes those two things are already true.
       The browser sentence is rendered from FolderPickDisplay rather than
       restated, so this lead and Home's line beside the pick button say the
       identical thing; the file names come from the constants the stores
       actually write, the same discipline the caps and stats-file prose use. *@
    <h2 class="h4 mt-4">Before you start</h2>
    <ul>
        <li>
            <strong>Your own analyzed positions.</strong> BgQuiz quizzes you on
            <code>.xg</code> match files and <code>.xgp</code> position files
            that you already have. It ships none of its own, so you need a folder
            of them on this machine before anything below applies.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>A supported browser.</strong> @FolderPickDisplay.SupportedBrowsers
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Two small files, written into that folder.</strong> BgQuiz
            saves <code>@QuizStatsFile.FileName</code> (your lifetime record) and
            <code>@QuizFiltersFile.FileName</code> (filters you save by name)
            beside your problem files. It writes nothing else, and never changes
            the files it quizzes you on.
        </li>
    </ul>

    @* Data ownership and storage, directly after the prerequisites and ahead of
       the flow: a reader who wants to know what happens to their positions wants
       that answer before handing over a folder, not twelve sections later. The
       "Nothing leaves your machine" bullet that used to close the list above was
       *moved* here rather than copied — the same page asserting it twice is
       exactly the duplication a consolidated section is prone to.

       The browser entries are rendered from the constants that write them
       (MixDraft.StorageKey, QuizSettings.StorageKey, QuizLiveMarker.StorageKey),
       never typed as prose
       literals, so a name a reader checks in devtools cannot drift from the name
       the code stores under — the discipline the file names and pick caps already
       use. The two files BgQuiz writes into the folder are deliberately NOT
       restated: they are named from their own constants in the list above, and
       this section points back at them.

       The filter panel's storage is a POINTER, not a description. XgFilter_Razor
       owns that prose and renders it from the panel's own constants inside
       FilterHelp (#fh-what-is-remembered), which this page already embeds under
       Choose filters. Naming or describing the panel's keys here would be a
       second encoding of a producer's internals that no test in this repo could
       notice drifting — the same ruling that removed this page's facet prose. *@
    <h2 class="h4 mt-4">Your data stays yours</h2>
    <p>
        Your positions and matches are yours, and BgQuiz treats them that way. It
        reads the files you point it at and parses them inside your browser; they
        are never uploaded, and neither are the positions in them, the answers you
        give, or your scores. There is no account to create, and the server BgQuiz
        is downloaded from has nothing to receive any of it — it hands your browser
        the app and plays no further part.
    </p>
    <p>
        BgQuiz does keep three small entries in the browser itself, so your setup is
        still there when you come back:
    </p>
    <ul>
        <li>
            <code>@MixDraft.StorageKey</code> — the weighted mix you last applied,
            in this site's local storage. It survives reloading the page and
            closing the browser, and it holds the mix settings only: the
            categories, their percentages and order, and the quiz length.
        </li>
        <li>
            <code>@QuizSettings.StorageKey</code> — your choices from the
            <em>Settings</em> page, also in this site's local storage. It holds
            those settings and nothing else, and it too survives reloading and
            closing the browser.
        </li>
        <li>
            <code>@QuizLiveMarker.StorageKey</code> — a single mark noting that a
            quiz was running, which is how BgQuiz can tell you afterwards that a
            reload reset it. It is kept in <em>session</em> storage, which belongs
            to this browser tab alone: another tab never sees it, and it
            disappears when you close this one.
        </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
        You can see all three under this site's storage in your browser's developer
        tools, and delete them there whenever you like. The filter panel keeps its
        own settings in your browser as well —
        <a href="@PanelStorageHref">What the panel remembers</a>, in
        <em>Choose filters</em> below, lists exactly what those are.
    </p>
    <p>
        Everything else BgQuiz keeps, it writes into your own problem folder: the
        two files named above, and nothing besides.
    </p>

    <h2 class="h4 mt-4">Pick your folder</h2>
    <p>
        On the home page, choose the local folder holding your <code>.xg</code> match
        files and <code>.xgp</code> position files. Only files directly in that folder
        are used — subfolders are ignored. A folder can hold up to
        @PickedFileLimits.MaxFileCount problem files, each up to
        @(PickedFileLimits.MaxFileMegabytes) MB.
    </p>

    <h2 class="h4 mt-4">Choose filters</h2>
    @* The panel's information hierarchy is user-visible, so the tour has to
       describe it: a reader who sees only an error range needs telling that the
       rest exists and where it went. Named in the panel's own words (Show more
       filters / Clear filters) so the page and the control can be matched up at
       a glance. The hidden-active signal is called out because it is the answer
       to "why is my quiz smaller than I expect" when a filter is in force out
       of sight.

       Which filters those are, and what each one admits, is deliberately not
       written here — FilterHelp is embedded at the end of this section and
       names every facet from the lib's own labels. This paragraph carries only
       what FilterHelp cannot know: where the controls sit in *this* app's
       panel, and that the disclosure remembers its state. The facet list this
       paragraph used to recite was a hand-maintained copy of the panel's
       sections, which is the drift the embed exists to end. *@
    <p>
        The filter panel narrows which decisions the quiz will ask you. The
        <em>error range</em> is the control most people reach for, so it sits at
        the top and is always shown; every other filter sits behind
        <em>Show more filters</em>, and opening it once is remembered for next
        time. While it is closed the button carries a count and names the hidden
        sections that currently hold a filter, so nothing you set can quietly
        stay in force out of sight.
    </p>
    <p>
        Filters must be <strong>applied</strong> before Start becomes available —
        editing any control un-applies them until you press <em>Apply Filter</em>
        again. Opening or closing <em>Show more filters</em> is not an edit, so it
        never un-applies anything. <em>Clear filters</em> puts every control back to
        its default and applies that, leaving the quiz unfiltered. Optionally tick
        <em>Shuffle order</em> to see positions in a random order rather than in
        file order.
    </p>
    @* The count line, previously undocumented anywhere. Two things about it are
       easy to read wrongly — it counts matching *decisions* rather than problems
       you will be shown, and it describes the filters alone — and the second is
       load-bearing once a mix is applied, since the quiz can then be far smaller
       than the number on screen. Home states that caveat beside the number in its
       own voice; this stays prose, per FolderPickDisplay's "Help's prose stays
       prose" rule (a shared constant is earned only by two surfaces rendering the
       same sentence). *@
    <p>
        Each time you press Apply, a line below the panel says how many decisions
        your filters matched. That is the pool your quiz is drawn from — and it
        counts <em>decisions</em>, not problems you will be shown: a position whose
        dice leave no legal play is counted here and then skipped for you at quiz
        time. It also describes the filters only. So if you have applied a weighted
        mix, the mix draws the quiz from that pool rather than presenting all of
        it, and the quiz can be much smaller than the number shown. Starting is
        what composes the mix, and the quiz page then tells you exactly what it
        drew.
    </p>
    @* The answer-type breakdown that rides under the count. It answers a
       question the count cannot — what the collection is made of, rather than
       how big it is — so it earns its own paragraph instead of a clause, and
       that paragraph leads with the zero reading, which is the one a reader
       would otherwise mistake for a rendering fault.

       The five bucket names are deliberately NOT recited here. They are copy
       owned by AnswerTypeDisplay and rendered on Home; a list of them in this
       prose would be a second encoding that drifts silently the first time a
       label is reworded — the same reasoning that removed this page's facet
       list. What this paragraph carries is what the labels cannot say for
       themselves: that the list is exhaustive, and what a zero means. *@
    <p>
        Under that number, BgQuiz breaks the pool down by the kind of answer its
        decisions call for — checker plays, and each of the four cube verdicts.
        Every kind is listed every time, so a kind showing
        <strong>0</strong> is a result rather than an omission: it says your
        folder holds none of that kind under the filters you applied. If you
        suspect your saved positions lean one way, this is where it shows.
    </p>

    @* Per-facet reference, rendered by XgFilter_Razor's own FilterHelp rather
       than restated here. The facets are the panel's behavior, and the panel is
       the producer's; a description of them written on this page would be a
       second encoding of lib rules that drifts the moment those rules change,
       with no test anywhere able to notice. The depth facet's redesign into
       per-mode clauses is what settled it: BgQuiz was spared only because it
       had never described that facet, which is luck, not a discipline. The
       producer's Pitfalls make it a rule — a host embeds FilterHelp and adds
       app-level framing only, and prose FilterHelp lacks is extended there,
       never written here.

       Placed last in the section so the app's own framing — where the controls
       live, that Apply gates Start, what the count means — reads as continuous
       prose before the reference block. FilterHelp brings its own headings
       (an h4 lead and one h5 per facet), which sit below this page's h2s and so
       leave the pinned section skeleton alone. It is render-only and
       parameterless: no state, no JS, nothing for this page to wire. *@
    <FilterHelp />

    <h2 class="h4 mt-4">Save filters you use often</h2>
    <p>
        Above the filter panel, <em>Saved Filters</em> keeps named copies of
        filter settings you want back later — a race-position set, one opponent,
        a particular band of mistake sizes. Type a name and press <em>Save</em>
        to store the filters as they currently stand; <em>Load</em> puts a saved
        set back into the panel below, and <em>Delete</em> removes one (both
        overwriting and deleting ask you to confirm first). A loaded set arrives
        as an edit, not as a decision — Start stays disabled until you press
        Apply, so you are free to adjust it first.
    </p>
    <p>
        Saved filters live in a file named <code>@QuizFiltersFile.FileName</code>
        inside your problem folder, so they belong to that folder and travel with
        it. They need the same write access lifetime stats need: without it you
        can still load filters saved earlier, but not save, change, or delete
        them. If the file can't be read, BgQuiz leaves it exactly as it found it
        and switches saving off rather than risk overwriting it.
    </p>

    <h2 class="h4 mt-4">Weight the quiz by your lifetime stats</h2>
    <p>
        Below the filters, <em>Weighted mix</em> composes the quiz out of your
        <em>Lifetime stats</em> (below) instead of simply taking everything the
        filters matched. Add categories — never seen, ever got wrong, not seen in
        so many days, average equity loss over a threshold, wrong more than some
        share of the time — give each a percentage, and make the percentages
        total 100. You get a quiz built to those proportions from the problems
        your filters selected.
    </p>
    <ul>
        <li>
            <strong>Row order settles overlap.</strong> A problem that fits two
            of your categories counts toward the <em>earlier</em> row, so the
            ↑ / ↓ buttons are how you say which category gets first claim.
            <em>Everything else</em> catches whatever the rows above it didn't.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Quiz length is optional.</strong> Left blank, the mix takes
            every problem its categories can reach; set it and the percentages
            divide up that many problems instead.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>An applied mix owns the order.</strong> Its own
            <em>Random order</em> setting then decides how problems are
            presented, and the page's Shuffle order checkbox is disabled — your
            choice there is kept, and comes back when you reset the mix.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>No lifetime stats, no mix.</strong> The mix is offered only
            for a folder pick that can save stats, because that record is what it
            composes from. If the stats turn out to be unusable when you press
            Start, BgQuiz refuses rather than quietly running an unweighted quiz:
            it offers to start that one quiz without the mix, and keeps the mix
            you built either way.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Apply it like a filter.</strong> While the mix shown differs
            from the mix applied, Start stays disabled until you press
            <em>Apply Mix</em> or <em>Reset</em>. Your edits keep — visiting
            another page and coming back doesn't lose them.
        </li>
    </ul>

    <h2 class="h4 mt-4">Answer the position</h2>
    <p>
        Each problem is one of two kinds, and the board tells you which:
    </p>
    <ul>
        <li>
            <strong>Checker play.</strong> The dice are rolled, and you enter the play you
            would make by clicking the board — see <em>Making a checker play</em> below.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Cube decision.</strong> The board is read-only. Answer with the radio
            buttons beside the Submit button: what the player on roll should do
            (double or not), and how the opponent should respond (take or pass).
        </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
        Press <em>Submit</em> to answer, or <em>Skip</em> to move on without answering.
    </p>

    <h2 class="h4 mt-4">Making a checker play</h2>
    <p>
        Plays are entered with single clicks — there is nothing to drag, and no
        pick-up-and-put-down. The board updates after every click, and only clicks that keep
        a legal full play reachable are accepted — anything else simply does nothing, so you
        cannot build an illegal play, and you cannot leave part of the roll unplayed when it
        could have been played. Landing on a lone opposing checker hits it automatically.
    </p>
    <ul>
        <li>
            <strong>Clicking on a point you occupy moves a checker from there:</strong> it
            advances by one die straight away. When either die could play from that point, the
            <em>leftmost</em> die shown on the board is used — click the dice to swap them
            (see below). When the move carries the checker off the board, it bears off; that
            is just an ordinary move.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Clicking on a point that's empty or contains an opposing blot brings
            checkers there.</strong> If the point can be made, it is: two of your checkers land
            on it in the fewest moves, hitting anything in the way. With ordinary dice that
            uses both and finishes the play; with doubles you may still have dice left. If the
            point cannot be made, one checker that can reach it goes there instead. When there
            are two equally short ways to do it, the click does nothing; make the moves one at
            a time instead. This works even while you have a checker on the bar — see
            <em>Clicking on the bar</em>.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Clicking on the bar enters a checker</strong>, and always works while you
            have one there. When both dice could enter, the <em>leftmost</em> die is used —
            swap the dice to enter on the other point, or click the entry point directly. While
            any checker of yours is on the bar it must enter before anything else can move —
            but you can still click any point that is empty or holds an opposing blot: the
            entry happens first, and the rest of the roll brings a checker to the point you
            clicked. That checker need not be the one that entered — with a 2-1 from the bar,
            clicking an opposing blot in your home board enters with the two and hits with the
            one, as long as there is exactly one way to do it. Clicking a point you occupy does
            nothing while you are on the bar, even one the entering checker could reach: enter
            first, then move normally.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Clicking on the checker tray bears off as many checkers as the roll
            allows</strong>, whenever there is a unique way to do it. If two ways bear off
            equally many, the tray does nothing — bear off point by point instead, by clicking
            each checker's own point.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Click the dice to swap them, or to submit.</strong> While the play is
            unfinished, clicking the dice swaps the order they are shown in — which changes
            which one a checker click uses — without disturbing the moves you have already
            made. (Doubles have nothing to swap.) Once the play is finished, clicking the dice
            submits it, exactly as the <em>Submit</em> button does.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Change your mind — <em>Undo last</em> and <em>Undo all</em>.</strong> Undo
            last takes back the most recent move; Undo all restores the starting position.
            <em>Submit</em> stays disabled until the whole roll has been played.
        </li>
    </ul>

    <h2 class="h4 mt-4">Scoring</h2>
    <p>
        A submitted answer is scored against XG's analysis of that position. Correct means
        you chose a play or action with no equity loss; otherwise the equity you gave up is
        recorded. Skipped problems are counted separately and never affect your accuracy.
        Scores are kept in four buckets: checker plays, doubling decisions, take decisions,
        and the total across all three.
    </p>

    <h2 class="h4 mt-4">Review the solution</h2>
    <p>
        After you submit, the board switches to the solution view: XG's full analysis panel,
        with the recorded play marked <strong>*</strong> and your answer marked
        <strong>†</strong> when it differs. <em>Continue</em> moves to the next problem;
        <em>Redo</em> takes back your answer and returns you to the same position to try
        again.
    </p>

    <h2 class="h4 mt-4">Stats and finishing</h2>
    <p>
        <em>Show stats</em> opens a running scoreboard at any point mid-quiz, and returns you
        to exactly where you were. When the last problem is answered you land on the summary
        page, where you can restart with the same filters or start over with new ones.
    </p>

    <h2 class="h4 mt-4">Lifetime stats</h2>
    <p>
        In browsers that can grant write access to a folder (Chrome and Edge), BgQuiz
        also keeps a <em>lifetime</em> record: how often each position has been quizzed,
        how often you got it right, and when you last saw it. It lives in a small file
        named <code>@QuizStatsFile.FileName</code> inside your problem folder, right
        beside the files it tracks, and is updated after each problem you answer.
        Like your problem files, it never leaves your machine — it is written directly
        to your own folder, and nothing is uploaded anywhere.
    </p>
    <ul>
        <li>
            <strong>Only answered problems count.</strong> Skips, plays XG never listed,
            and positions skipped for you are not part of your lifetime record. A redone
            answer counts once — the answer you continue past.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>A cube position records two decisions.</strong> The double and the
            take are counted separately, the same way the in-quiz score counts them — so
            getting one half right and the other wrong reads as one correct out of two,
            not as a single wrong answer.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Other browsers still quiz.</strong> Where the browser can't save into
            the folder (Firefox, Safari), or write access isn't granted, the quiz itself is
            unchanged — the same problems, the same filters, the same scoreboard. What is
            lost is the record that builds up <em>across</em> sessions of which problems
            give you difficulty: nothing is written, so nothing accumulates. The home page
            tells you which applies when you pick.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>An unreadable stats file is left alone.</strong> If the file can't be
            read (edited by hand, or written by a newer BgQuiz), the quiz runs without
            recording and the file is not touched.
        </li>
    </ul>

    <h2 class="h4 mt-4">Things worth knowing</h2>
    <ul>
        <li>
            <strong>Positions with no legal play are skipped for you.</strong> When the dice
            leave the player on roll nothing to do, the position is passed over silently —
            you never see it, and it does not count as a skip.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>A play XG never listed counts as a skip, not a wrong answer.</strong> If
            you submit a legal play that isn't among the analyzed candidates, there is no
            equity loss to record against it, so it scores as a skip. You still see the
            solution.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>A cube position is two decisions, not one.</strong> The double and the
            take are scored independently, so one cube problem contributes one doubling
            decision and one take decision to your totals.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Clicking the dice advances.</strong> On the solution view, clicking the
            dice on the board does exactly what <em>Continue</em> does.
        </li>
        @* Deliberately about the control and its behaviour, never an inventory of
           what the panel holds — that list grows, and prose naming its entries
           would be wrong the day one is added. *@
        <li>
            <strong>The side panel folds out of the way.</strong> On a wide screen, the
            narrow strip down the left edge folds the blue panel away and hands its width
            back to the page — worth a click if the quiz feels crowded. Clicking the strip
            again brings the panel back, and the arrow on the strip always points the way
            the panel will move. The fold stays put while you work through the quiz —
            answering, skipping and continuing all leave it alone — and the panel comes
            back when you move to another page or reload. If you would rather it did
            not come back, turn on <em>Keep the navigation panel folded</em> on the
            <em>Settings</em> page.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Reloading the browser starts you over.</strong> Moving between pages
            inside the app is always safe — your folder, filters, and progress are kept. But a
            full browser reload (F5) discards all of it, and you will need to pick your folder
            again. Your lifetime stats file is not lost — it lives in your folder, and picking
            the folder again resumes it.
        </li>
    </ul>

    @* Feedback. Same href as Home's footer link, from the one AppInfo value, so
       the version in the subject can never be the version of some other build.
       Naming the version in the prose too is deliberate: a tester who mails from
       a different device still knows what to quote. *@
    <h2 class="h4 mt-4">Send feedback</h2>
    <p>
        BgQuiz is in beta, and the point of this round is to hear what breaks,
        what confuses, and what is missing.
        <a href="@AppInfo.FeedbackMailto">Email the BgQuiz beta address</a> — the
        subject line comes pre-filled with the exact build you are running
        (<code>@AppInfo.Version</code>), which is the first thing needed to make
        sense of a report.
    </p>

    @if (Controller.HasStarted && !Controller.IsFinished)
    {
        <button class="btn btn-primary mt-3" @onclick="BackToQuiz">
            Back to quiz
        </button>
    }
</div>
